## 9. Quantitative Results

### 9.1 Main comparison (RQ1)

[[TAB:tab05_results_mc7|tab:mc7]]

Table \ref{tab:mc7} reports seven-way results on the official MC-Hinglish1.0 test split. The character-n-gram TF-IDF
SVM reaches a test macro-F1 of {{ v.mc7_test.mc7_tfidf_svm.macro_f1 }} and TF-IDF logistic regression
{{ v.mc7_test.mc7_tfidf_lr.macro_f1 }}. The fine-tuned transformers are **lower** on test: MuRIL
{{ v.mc7_test.mc7_muril_base.macro_f1 }} ± {{ v.mc7_test.mc7_muril_base.macro_f1_sd }}, XLM-R
{{ v.mc7_test.mc7_xlmr_base.macro_f1 }} ± {{ v.mc7_test.mc7_xlmr_base.macro_f1_sd }}, and HingRoBERTa
{{ v.mc7_test.mc7_hingroberta_base.macro_f1 }} ± {{ v.mc7_test.mc7_hingroberta_base.macro_f1_sd }} (mean ± s.d. over three
seeds). The ordering holds on the {{ v.n_test_dedup }}-item de-duplicated test subset (TF-IDF SVM
{{ v.mc7_test_dedup.mc7_tfidf_svm.macro_f1 }}; MuRIL {{ v.mc7_test_dedup.mc7_muril_base.macro_f1 }}), so it is not an
artefact of near-duplicates. Near-duplicates favour the TF-IDF models: their macro-F1 on the 182 near-duplicate items
({{ v.mc7_test_neardup.mc7_tfidf_svm.macro_f1 }} for the SVM) exceeds that on the de-duplicated items, whereas MuRIL scores
{{ v.mc7_test_neardup.mc7_muril_base.macro_f1 }} on near-duplicates (Table \ref{tab:subsets}).

[[TAB:tab05b_mc7_subsets|tab:subsets]]

On the **validation** split, which is carved from the official training data, the picture differs. Transformers and
TF-IDF are close: MuRIL reaches a validation macro-F1 of {{ v.mc7_val.mc7_muril_base.macro_f1 }}, TF-IDF SVM
{{ v.mc7_val.mc7_tfidf_svm.macro_f1 }}, and XLM-R {{ v.mc7_val.mc7_xlmr_base.macro_f1 }}. The transformers therefore
lose much more between validation and test than the linear models do.

### 9.2 Train–test distribution shift

[[TAB:tab03b_split_shift|tab:shift]]

To explain the validation–test gap, we trained a domain classifier (TF-IDF logistic regression, 5-fold
cross-validation) to tell training texts from test texts. It separates the official training and test splits with
ROC-AUC {{ v.shift.train_vs_test }}, and within every class the AUC is between {{ v.shift.min_class }} and
{{ v.shift.max_class }}. The same classifier separates training from our validation split only at chance-like levels
(AUC {{ v.shift.train_vs_val }}; Table \ref{tab:shift}). The official test split is therefore drawn from a noticeably
different distribution than the training split, consistent with a separately collected batch. Validation scores
consequently overestimate performance on the official test data, more so for the fine-tuned transformers than for
character-n-gram models. We return to possible reasons in Section 14.

### 9.3 Per-class behaviour

[[TAB:tab06_per_class_mc7|tab:perclass]]

[[FIG:results/figures/evaluation/fig11_confusion_mc7|Confusion matrices on the official MC7 test set (counts; shading is row-normalised recall).|fig:confusion]]

Target-defined classes are easiest for MuRIL: `age` (F1 {{ v.mc7_test.mc7_muril_base.per_class.age.f1 }}) and
`religion` ({{ v.mc7_test.mc7_muril_base.per_class.religion.f1 }}). The pragmatically or lexically defined classes
are hardest: `mockery` ({{ v.mc7_test.mc7_muril_base.per_class.mockery.f1 }}) and `offensive`
({{ v.mc7_test.mc7_muril_base.per_class.offensive.f1 }}). Most confusions involve these two classes, for example
offensive→gender and mockery→not_cyberbullying (Figure \ref{fig:confusion}). This pattern matches the dataset's mixed
label semantics: an offensive remark about a woman is plausibly both "offensive" and "gender".

